// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// specs/t27f/token_floor.t27 -- an infographic in which every particle carries a fact: what one
// token of a chat reply costs in energy, set against the least energy physics allows for it.
//
// WHY: the owner, 2026-10-18, on the engine reel: the particles there fly but carry no information;
// where is the infographic built on this technology? This is it. The numbers come from
// specs/physics/physical_limits.t27, the forces from specs/t27f/particle_engine.t27, and the frame
// helpers from specs/t27f/reel_particles.t27. Nothing here restates any of them.
//
// THE FACT, as physical_limits computes it:
//     the floor   picking one token out of a 128256-token vocabulary names 17 bits; erasing 17 bits
//                 at 300 K costs at least 17 k T ln 2 = 4.9e-20 J (R. Landauer, IBM J. Res. Dev. 5,
//                 183, 1961)
//     the bill    0.24 Wh for a median text prompt (Google, 2025, arXiv:2508.15734), over a reply of
//                 500 tokens: 1.7 J a token
//     the gap     1.7 J / 4.9e-20 J = 3.5e19: 19 whole decades. The bill is engineering, not
//                 thermodynamics.
// The vocabulary size and the reply length are UNVERIFIED illustrative inputs in physical_limits,
// and the picture says so in its footer.
//
// THE PICTURE: a glass tube stands on a glossy black floor. Twenty glass balls drop into it one by
// one. The first, white, is the floor: 4.9e-20 J. Each gold ball after it multiplies by ten, so the
// column is a logarithmic bar chart that builds itself: 19 gold balls, one per decade of the gap.
// The tube holds the balls in line, so their motion is one-dimensional: gravity g = G_STD, Hertz
// contact between neighbours and with the floor (contact_f_over_r) with the engine's dashpot
// C_CONTACT, integrated by velocity Verlet at STEPS substeps of H per frame. Air drag on a 34 mm
// ball at these speeds is under 5 percent of its weight and is left out.
// Every letter is made of particles, set in t27.ai's own display face, Outfit ExtraBold
// (specs/t27f/outfit_atlas.t27): the outline sampled 12 dots to the cap height, each glyph as wide
// as its advance, and a dot's light the share of its point the outline covers. Each dot is a body
// steered to its place by the engine's critically damped PD controller (KP, KD): control enters as a force,
// never as a position. The labels of a ball are emitted by that ball at the frame the integrator
// finds it touching the stack: the floor label from the white ball, the decade tick beside each
// ball from the ball itself. The exponent of the counter "X10^k" is one pool of particles that
// re-forms into k each time gold ball k lands: the dots k needs beyond k - 1 leave ball k, the ones
// it no longer needs go out where they stand. A ball lands every 0.3 s, so the counter steers with
// the same controller made five times as fast, KP 25 and KD 5 times over, still critically damped.
// What the light does is derived from the state, as in the engine: a dot and a ball glow by
// glow_of(v); the floor mirrors everything with the Fresnel reflectance of lacquer at the angle
// the engine's camera sees it (mirror_alpha); light adds and the exposure 1 - exp(-L) never clips.
//
// THE FILM (specs/t27f/token_floor_scenes.t27 and specs/t27f/pbr_sphere.t27): the owner,
// 2026-10-18, asked for real-looking balls, a story told in shots instead of one still frame, and
// a horizontal and a vertical cut of the same reel. The stage above is built and run in world px
// exactly as before; each frame the camera of its shot maps it to the screen: eight shots, and
// between them no cut but a drone's flight, one smooth path that pulls back on a long way, as the
// owner asked after the first cut ("too jerky; fly like a drone"). Every moving splat is swept
// over the half frame a 180-degree shutter is open, so a falling ball, a flying letter and the
// flying camera smear as a camera sees them. A label the camera carries past the safe rect fades
// out whole (edge_fit), so no line is left half under the card or cut by the frame. A ball is
// shaded by pbr_sphere: GGX gold or glazed ceramic under four softboxes, with the ball above and
// the one below reflected in it and shadowing it. A landing flashes at the contact; a word's dots start left to right, one
// frame each STAGGER_PX of its width; a glass card with a gold edge and a PASS pill rises over the
// three verdict beats; the lens adds a vignette and half a code value of dither before rounding.
//
// THE OUTPUT: `render(o)` writes FRAMES frames of frame_w(o) x frame_h(o) raw RGB24 to stderr, row
// by row, as specs/t27f/reel_particles.t27 does. `main` writes the 1080x1920 cut, then the
// 1920x1080 cut, the same number of bytes each; split the stream in half and read each with plain
// flags (`-f rawvideo -pix_fmt rgb24 -s 1080x1920 -r 30`, `-s 1920x1080` for the second).
// Each row of a frame visits only the splats a per-frame row index lists for it, in the order they
// were splatted (#10391); the raster's own check (touches_row) still decides, so the index only
// skips rows a splat cannot light and the bytes are those of a scan over every splat. A frame whose
// index would overflow INDEX_CAP is scanned over every splat instead, so a close-up can never write
// different bytes. The empty scene is not cached across frames as it was for the fixed camera
// (#10407): the camera moves the backdrop and the lens dithers it, so it differs in every frame.
// To use more cores, `main` renders shard k of N (environment TF_SHARD and TF_SHARDS; unset, 0 of
// 1, the whole reel) of the cut TF_CUT names (0 the tall cut, 1 the wide one; unset, both, tall
// first): the frames specs/t27f/frame_shard.t27 gives shard k of N. Frame f's state is the
// integral from frame 0, so every shard steps the physics from frame 0 and skips only the splats
// and the raster before its range. The shards of one N and one cut tile [0, FRAMES) in order, so their outputs concatenated
// are that cut's one-process output byte for byte. Unchanged frames need no render at all:
// specs/t27f/frame_cache.t27 keys them by this spec's seal.
//
// NOT CLAIMED: the ball mass, the drop height, the tube and the light levels are design choices,
// labelled as such. The balls are not a model of any computer; they are a bar chart that obeys
// Newton.
// phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

